s  are going back to  school and they're getting tested as  well again imagine if you weren't doing  that imagine if i wasn't testing  the hauliers the the lorry drivers um  going over to  the continent we wouldn't have been able  to reopen the border that was closed  uh just before christmas so to say that  the whole test and trace thing has  been a waste of time is is factually  untrue we wouldn't have goods flowing we  wouldn't have  medicines being imported because the  entire export import system would have  um frozen up  and then the the other very good example  of where it's worked is has been with  regards to these  variants uh i think i'm right in saying  the number of  variants from south africa has been  limited to 285  we know that because of test and trace  the number uh from brazil i think was  six  uh we know that because of test and  trace so it's been very successful in  doing things which otherwise would have  made would have meant that those  variants which are now the main concern  for us would have gotten out of control  so look i mean i didn't forget  we did have some difficulties finding  the sixth uh brazilian uh case of the  the brazilian variant but but just one  last point in this because obviously we  have much to talk about on your brief  a lot of people will look at and it's in  part you might think it's an unfair  comparison the one percent payoff  that has been made to nhs staff and the  2.7 million pounds being spent on  management consultants for test and  trace every day look everyone  understands there is there is a role for  consultancy  within any government people might  suggest that 3 million pounds a day is  just a little bit to true  yeah i mean look you can always look at  any budget and go why are you doing this  but you're not doing that or how are you  doing this bid but not that bit  i think um the nhs has had 60 billion  pounds more in the last year it's got  another 20 billion pounds  this year being spent on it because  we've got these extraordinary  extraordinary pressures um on the nhs  and without hesitation um this  government has provided everything the  nhs  needs to help get us through this um  through this pandemic i've seen it  firsthand  my dad's been in hospital and now  fortunately in rehab for  just over three months now including  having coronavirus in hospital during  that time  the nhs has been absolutely  extraordinary i've seen it firsthand um  i'm sure we can pick about uh pick apart  the budgets and say why this why not  that but  overall i i i just i i think the nhs has  performed phenomenally well  and been extraordinary over this period  i will do pass on our very best wishes  to your father i hope you get  back back to full speed um very soon  let's talk about the announcement from  the department for transport today you  know  millions of pounds being set aside  essentially to do some kind of  proprietary work for some for some quite  big projects and projects that i think  many people will see as  perhaps linked to to the future of the  union  well we certainly think it's a good idea  if you can get around the united kingdom  the union  and you can do so easily and what we've  done is some  in this interim report that's out today  um is identified a number of different  projects which  for whatever reasons aren't particularly  focused on  within each different constituent part  of the union in other words england  scotland wales and northern ireland but  actually  somebody needs to look at overall and  say ah this would help to make it easier  to travel from  for example northern ireland to england  which happens to be via road  called the a75 which is in scotland  that's never going to be a particular  priority for the scottish government but  actually it matters for connecting us  all together  making it easier for us to see our  friends and family and do business um  together so  that's the purpose of this connectivity  report uh the interim version